@module rooms
$9B37 b Grid0 9 ; Grid 0's record (stage 3: the longship): 47 x 9 blocks, the start window at block column 11, row 0, the room table Grid0Rooms, map offset 0, +8 = 6
> A grid record, 9 bytes, as in stage 1: +0 the width and +1 the height in blocks, +2/+3 the start window's block column and row (where StartGrid $A639 puts the window; the grid passages rewrite them), +4/+5 the room table, +6/+7 the grid's offset in the map section of the unpacked graphics, +8 the rooms a grid row of rooms (only the moves up and down read it; every stage-3 grid is one row of rooms, 9 blocks high, so +1 and +8 are read by no recording: grid 0's +8 = 6 does not match its 4 rooms and is never used). A room is a window of 14 x 9 blocks; the windows step 11 block columns, so neighbouring rooms share 3 columns of blocks. RoomGrid ($FE23) points at the record of the hero's grid (StageEntry sets $9B37); RoomCells reads +0, +6/+7.
> The room table, one byte a room: the exits, bit 0 right, bit 1 left, bit 2 up, bit 3 down. Grid 0: 4 rooms (1, 3, 3, 2: the deck from the stern to the prow); grid 1: 11 rooms (the swamp and the forest, right to the castle); grid 2: 1 room with no exit (the castle hall).
> Read in rzx-r3 and s3-right (StageEntry, RoomCells, StartGrid, the edge checks); grid 1's start column $9B46 is written once in rzx-r3 (the passage from grid 0). rooms.txt holds the three records, their room tables and the placement lists; tools/fig_rooms.py draws the map and every room from it (docs/img/maps/stage3*.png).
